# Hypersensitive dual-function luminescence switching of a silver-chalcogenolate cluster-based metal–organic framework

## abstract
Silver(i) chalcogenide/chalcogenolate clusters are promising photofunctional materials for sensing, optoelectronics and solar energy harvesting applications. However, their instability and poor room-temperature luminescent quantum yields have hampered more extensive study. Here, we graft such clusters to adaptable bridging ligands, enabling their interconnection and the formation of rigid metal-organic frameworks. By controlling the spatial separation and orientation of the clusters, they then exhibit enhanced stability (over one year) and quantum yield (12.1%). Ultrafast dual-function fluorescence switching (<1 s) is also achieved, with turn-off triggered by O2 and multicoloured turn-on by volatile organic compounds. Single-crystal X-ray diffraction of the inclusion materials, obtained by single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformation, enables precise determination of the position of the small molecules within the framework, elucidating the switching mechanism. The work enriches the cluster-based metal-organic framework portfolio, bridges the gap between silver chalcogenide/chalcogenolate clusters and metal-organic frameworks, and provides a foundation for further development of functional silver-cluster-based materials.

Silver(I) chalcogenide/chalcogenolate clusters are promising photofunctional materials for sensing, optoelectronics and solar energy harvesting applications. However, their instability and poor room-temperature luminescent quantum yields have hampered more extensive study. Here, we graft such clusters to adaptable bridging ligands, enabling their interconnection and the formation of rigid metal–organic frameworks. By controlling the spatial separation and orientation of the clusters, they then exhibit enhanced stability (over one year) and quantum yield $(12.1\%)$ . Ultrafast dual-function fluorescence switching $\bf(<1\mathsf{s})$ is also achieved, with turn-off triggered by $\bullet_{2}$ and multicoloured turn-on by volatile organic compounds. Single-crystal $\pmb{x}$ -ray diffraction of the inclusion materials, obtained by single-crystal-to-single-crystal transformation, enables precise determination of the position of the small molecules within the framework, elucidating the switching mechanism. The work enriches the cluster-based metal–organic framework portfolio, bridges the gap between silver chalcogenide/chalcogenolate clusters and metal–organic frameworks, and provides a foundation for further development of functional silver-cluster-based materials.
Metal–organic frameworks (MOFs) or porous coordination polymers (PCPs), in which organic ligands connect metal ion/cluster nodes via coordination bonds, have become a prominent class of crystalline materials in separation, storage, catalysis, chemical sensing and luminescent signalling devices13–17. Cluster-based nodes endow MOFs with enhanced stability and larger channels, and conveniently integrate the functionality of cluster units and organic linkers18–22. Consequently, the assembly of stable and functional cluster-based MOFs is a major pursuit in current crystal engineering, chemistry and materials science23,24. Strategies such as ‘reticular synthesis’25, the ‘supermolecular building block’ (SBB) approach26, ‘complexes as ligands’27,28 and ‘ligand exchanges’29 have been successfully developed for the designed synthesis of cluster-based MOFs.
Inspired by the characteristic features of SCCs and cluster-based MOFs, we reasoned that appropriate organic ligands might coordinately link and spatially separate SCCs in certain orientations to form luminescent SCC-based MOFs (SCC-MOFs). Such SCCMOFs might exhibit improved stability and luminescence properties through modulation of the SCC coordination shell by organic ligands and/or SCC embedment within a rigid framework5,8,30,31. In addition, photoluminescence of SCC-MOFs containing $d^{10}\ \mathrm{Ag}$ (I) clusters can arise from ligand-to-metal charge transfer (LMCT) or metal/cluster-to-ligand charge transfer (MLCT/CLCT)5,32,33 in response to external stimuli such as solvents, volatile organic compounds (VOCs), $\mathrm{\pH}$ and gases via host–guest interactions, thereby enabling applications involving luminescence sensing, molecular switches or chemical logic gates15,16,32–36.
## Results and discussion
The valuable features of $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ include its easy scale-up of synthesis, photostability, oxidation resistivity and chemical stability, which are key requirements in materials suitable for technological applications. Its inherently intense luminescence, moderate lifetime, robust porosity and observed change in incident fluorescence depending on its surroundings (air and solvent) will pave the way towards the development of solid-state chemical sensing and luminescent signalling devices.
Based on the above observations, $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ can function as an ultrafast dual-function switch, with ‘turn-off’ by $\mathrm{air}/\mathrm{O}_{2}$ quenching of green emission of $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ in vacuum, and multicolour ‘turnon’ by VOC without the effect of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ , leading to a two-input $(\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and VOC) IMPLICATION logic gate35,48 (truth table shown in Fig. 4c). VOC has priority in the multicolour ‘on’ state; otherwise, $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ predominates in the dark ‘off’ state, and there is another green ‘on’ state in vacuum. Such hypersensitive dual-function luminescence switching and an IMPLICATION logic gate have not been reported among MOFs and SCCs materials.
Obviously, the intercalation of VOCs occupies the channels and impedes contact of $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ with the host framework, which consequently turns on the emission of $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ . Its fast linear response is attributable to (1) easy ingress of VOCs into its robust cavity, while the host framework remains intact, (2) its intrinsic microporosity, which maximizes the framework–guest interaction within the confined space.15,16,32–34, and (3) a uniformly ordered arrangement of chromophores.
## Conclusion
In summary, we have explored silver(I) chalcogenide/chalcogenolate clusters as new building blocks to design and synthesize a lightemitting porous metal–organic framework. This novel SCC-MOF overcomes the problematic instability of SCCs to exhibit remarkably improved luminescence properties. Its permanent open channel provides a platform for various applications in host–guest chemistry. Unprecedented ultrafast dual-functional, turn-off fluorescence switching by $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ and multicoloured turn-on fluorescence switching by VOCs were obtained. Most excitingly, the switching mechanism was distinctly resolved by SCXRD structural analysis of $\mathrm{O}_{2}/\mathrm{VOCs}.$ including crystals. This work successfully demonstrates that the designed construction of SCC-MOFs opens a route to functional metal (Ag, Au, Cu, Pd)-cluster-based MOFs exhibiting enhanced stability and desirable photophysical properties for chemical sensing, catalysis and optoelectronic devices.
## Methods
## Preparation of $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ single crystals
Method 2: one-pot synthesis. $^{4,4^{\prime}}$ -Bipyridine (bpy) was directly added to the synthesis solution of $\mathbf{A}\mathbf{g}_{12}$ . $\mathrm{AgS}^{t}\mathrm{Bu}$ ( $_{0.60\ \mathrm{g}},$ 3 mmol), $\mathrm{CF}_{3}\mathrm{COOAg}$ $(0.45\mathrm{g},2\mathrm{mmol})$ and bpy $(0.50\mathrm{g},3\mathrm{mmol})$ were dissolved in a mixed solvent of $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CN}$ and $\mathrm{CH}_{3}\mathrm{CH}_{2}\mathrm{OH}$ (1:1) under stirring and subsequently filtered. The filtrate was slowly evaporated in air $(5-10~^{\circ}\mathrm{C})$ to give bulk colourless crystals that were rinsed with EtOH, filtered and dried in air to produce $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ in $75\%$ yield based on Ag.
Preparation of evacuated $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ single crystals. Following exposure of the $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ crystals to air for two days, a perfect single crystal was selected and carefully stuck in a capillary tube with one end sealed and the other connected to a BELSORPmax gas sorption analyser. The crystal-containing capillary tube was evacuated at $298\mathrm{~K~}$ until a vacuum pressure of $\mathrm{i}0^{-3}\ –10^{-4}$ Pa was achieved, and was retained for more than $^{12\mathrm{{h}}}$ . Subsequently, the open end was sealed $i n$ situ to maintain highvacuum conditions in the capillary tube, producing evacuated $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ . SCXRD data for evacuated $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ were then collected at 290 and $100~\mathrm{K}.$ .
Preparation of $\mathbf{Ag}_{12}\mathbf{bp}\mathbf{y}\mathbf{\cdot}\mathbf{O}_{2}$ single crystals. The same single crystal of evacuated $\mathrm{{Ag}_{12}\mathrm{{bpy}}}$ was carefully removed from the first capillary tube and stuck in another capillary tube, which was evacuated until a pressure of $10^{-3}–10^{-4}$ Pa was again achieved using the degassing methods described above. Next, pure pressurized $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ was backfilled at $77\mathrm{K}$ through the open end of the capillary until the pressure reached $30\mathrm{kPa}$ . Subsequently, the open end was sealed in situ to maintain the crystal under high pure $\mathrm{O}_{2}$ pressure surroundings, producing $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y{\cdot}O_{2}}$ , which was subjected to SCXRD analysis at 290 and $100~\mathrm{K}.$ .
Preparation of $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y{\cdot}V O C s}$ crystals. Crystals of $\mathbf{Ag_{12}b p y}$ were immersed in various liquid VOCs or were treated with VOCs vapour (ethanol, chloroform, acetone, acetonitrile, cyclohexane, dioxane, benzene, toluene, fluorobenzene, chlorobenzene, bromobenzene, iodobenzene, $o$ -xylene, $m$ -xylene and $\boldsymbol{p}$ -xylene) to give a series of isomorphous crystalline inclusion complexes.
